\section{What the instruction stream promises}

A virtual instruction set defines the state an instruction reads and the changes it may make. It need not define the state that the implementation stores. For instance, Lindholm et al.'s \emph{Java Virtual Machine Specification} gives each frame an operand stack and a local variable array; an implementation can keep a hot operand on a host register without making that register part of a class file. Conversely, a file format must nail down details an interpreter can otherwise leave implicit: field widths, encoding order, branch bases, and the meanings of invalid encodings. Once independent producers and consumers exist, changing an implicit convention is a compatibility change.

Start with the operations the language needs to specify precisely. Is addition modular, checked, or promoted? Does a conditional branch consume its condition? Where does an exception resume, and in what state? Specify call and return, reference identity, and memory operations before choosing a mnemonic inventory. In Paul Frenger's \emph{The Growing Machine, a Pre-Forth Language Implementation}, the Growing Machine uses separate stacks for operands, results, decisions, and return addresses. Frenger contrasts this with Forth's single parameter stack for operands and results. Both designs have implicit operands, but moving a value between successive operations costs different work depending on whether the operations use the same stack.

\section{Stack, register, and single-assignment designs}

A stack instruction can encode an arithmetic operation in one opcode byte because its sources and destination are implicit. Loading a local or using the same intermediate twice may still require explicit stack traffic. Register code instead names locations in a virtual frame, not host registers. It can reuse an intermediate by naming it again and may execute fewer opcodes, but fetching and decoding register indices costs space and time. Compact encodings can reserve short forms for commonly used registers. Widening a frame index must either select a prescribed wide form or fail; silent truncation makes different locals alias.

Jeffery von Ronne, Ning Wang, Alexander Apel, and Michael Franz demonstrate an alternative in \emph{A Virtual Machine for Interpreting Programs in Static Single Assignment Form}. Their interpretable SSA gives instructions result locations and records which control-flow edge arrived at a join, so the interpreter can select the corresponding $\phi$ operand. Multiple $\phi$ definitions at a join must see the predecessor values, not the results of earlier $\phi$ assignments on that same entry. A loop that swaps a pair of values is a useful test: a sequential overwrite can turn $(a,b)$ into $(b,b)$ instead of $(b,a)$. SSA makes dependence explicit (Chapter~\ref{ch:ssa}), but a direct interpreter must still implement edge selection and preserve simultaneous assignment.

\section{Worked comparison: two encodings, one result}

Take the expression $(a\times b)+a$ on two 32-bit integer inputs. Define arithmetic modulo $2^{32}$ and place the arguments in locals or registers 0 and 1. These sample instruction sets have one-byte opcodes and one-byte explicit operands:

\begin{verbatim}
Stack             Register
LOAD 0            MUL 2,0,1
LOAD 1            ADD 3,2,0
MUL               RET 3
LOAD 0
ADD
RET
\end{verbatim}

The stack code occupies nine bytes and dispatches six instructions; it needs at most two operand cells beyond its locals. The three register instructions occupy ten bytes and name a frame with four slots. The two variants need not differ in minimum storage: reusing register 2 as the destination of \texttt{ADD} is valid if the instruction reads both sources before writing. For $a=3,b=7$ both return 24; for $a=2^{32}-1,b=2$ both return $2^{32}-3$. That second test ensures the reference interpreter does not inherit the host language's signed-overflow rules.

