Compact operands save bytes only if readers agree exactly where the
next operation starts. Consider a toy external encoding with
\texttt{PUSH8} followed by one unsigned byte, \texttt{JZ8} followed by
one signed displacement byte, and one-byte \texttt{RET}. A branch uses
the end of its own encoding as base and consumes one integer on
either outcome. Decode the entire stream before resolving branch
targets. The result is a vector whose branch operands name decoded
operations, together with a reverse map for reporting external offsets.
This is structural decoding; in particular it does not prove that
the stack supplies an operand on every path.

\begin{sudocode}[Translate a relative offset into an operation index]
\State $p\gets 0$; $ops\gets []$; $index\gets\emptyset$
\While{$p<|bytes|$}
  \State $(op,a,w)\gets\Call{ReadComplete}{bytes,p}$ or reject
  \State $index[p]\gets|ops|$ \Comment{Operands are not entries}
  \State append $(op,a,p,w)$ to $ops$; $p\gets p+w$
\EndWhile
\ForAll{$(op,a,p,w)$ in $ops$ where $op$ is \texttt{JZ8}}
  \State $t\gets p+w+\Call{SignExtend}{a}$ \Comment{End-relative}
  \If{$t$ is not a key in $index$}
    \State \Return invalid target at $p$
  \EndIf
  \State replace branch operand by $index[t]$
\EndFor
\State \Return $(ops,index)$
\end{sudocode}

Consider starts at offsets 0, 2, 4, 6, and 7, containing
\texttt{PUSH8 7}, \texttt{PUSH8 0}, \texttt{JZ8 +1},
\texttt{RET}, and \texttt{RET}. The branch base is 6 and target 7,
which is decoded index 4 with zero-based indexing. It consumes the
zero and returns 7 at offset 7. With condition 1 instead, execution
returns 7 at offset 6; instrument the return position to distinguish
the edges. A displacement of $-1$ points to byte 5, the operand of
the branch, and decoding must reject it even if the condition is 1.

The invariant is that every decoded control destination denotes a
unique external start and can be mapped back without reparsing. Use
nonwrapping arithmetic for target computation. With direct offset
indexing, the two passes are linear in the stream length. A branch
to the end of the byte stream is not a return and must fail this
format's start check. Also test a final opcode missing its operand.
Before execution, check fall-through edges and stack effects as well
(Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-242}).

\section{Keep portable code separate from execution code}

Pick an external form using whole programs, not the shortest example. Compare bytes including branch operands, metadata, and constant pools, and count dispatched operations including moves, calls, and spills to virtual locals. Measure startup separately: decoding into a wider internal form can pay for long runs and still lose on short ones. A simple invariant for that translation is that every internal operation boundary at which the VM can stop has a mapping back to the corresponding external operation and its state. Use it to compare return values and exception locations under both interpreters, not just instruction counts. The Modern Compiler Dossier's Chapter~02, \emph{Multi-Level Interpreter}, develops the same distinction between a stable meaning and replaceable execution representations; Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-243} examines one reason to make that distinction even without a JIT.
